#: ../../../actors.md:1 ../../../actors.md:8
msgid "Actors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial we will learn how to add `actors` to our world and how to "
"create a scripted animation. You can find the final world of this tutorial "
"[here](https://github.com/ignitionrobotics/docs/blob/master/dome/tutorials/"
"actors/actor_demo.sdf)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:6
msgid ""
"Animations are very useful if we want to have entities following a "
"predefined path in simulation without being affected by the physics. This "
"means that they won't fall due to gravity or collide with other objects. "
"They will however, have a 3D visualization which can be seen by RGB cameras, "
"and 3D meshes which can be detected by GPU based sensors. The tutorial "
"explains how to create open-loop trajectories which don't interact with the "
"rest of the simulation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:10
msgid "In Gazebo Sim, an animated model is called an `actor`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:12
msgid ""
"There are two types of animations which can be used separately or combined "
"together:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:14
msgid ""
"Skeleton animation, which is relative motion between links in one model:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:16
msgid "![skeleton_movement](tutorials/actors/skeleton_movement.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:16
msgid "skeleton_movement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:18
msgid ""
"Trajectory animation, which carries all of the actor's links around the "
"world as one group along a trajectory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:20
msgid "![trajectory_movement](tutorials/actors/trajectory_movement.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:20
msgid "trajectory_movement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:22
msgid "Combined, to achieve a skeleton animation which moves in the world:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:24
msgid "![combined_movement](tutorials/actors/combined_movement.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:24
msgid "combined_movement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:26
msgid "Actors have the following properties:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:28
msgid ""
"No forces are applied on them, be it from gravity or contact or anything else"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:29
msgid ""
"Actors support skeleton animation imported from COLLADA (.dae) and BVH (."
"bvh) files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:30
msgid "Actors can have trajectories scripted directly in SDF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:32
msgid ""
"You can check out the full specification for the `<actor>` SDF element [here]"
"(http://sdformat.org/spec?ver=1.7&elem=actor)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:35
msgid "Skeleton"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:37
msgid ""
"Gazebo Sim supports two different skeleton animation file formats: COLLADA (."
"dae) and Biovision Hierarchy (.bvh)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:39
msgid ""
"Open any world, [empty.sdf](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/"
"ignitionrobotics/ign-gazebo/main/examples/worlds/empty.sdf) world for "
"example, and add an actor called `actor_walking` as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:42
msgid ""
"<actor name=\"actor_walking\">\n"
"    <skin>\n"
"        <filename>https://fuel.ignitionrobotics.org/1.0/Mingfei/models/actor/"
"tip/files/meshes/walk.dae</filename>\n"
"        <scale>1.0</scale>\n"
"    </skin>\n"
"    <animation name=\"walk\">\n"
"        <filename>https://fuel.ignitionrobotics.org/1.0/Mingfei/models/actor/"
"tip/files/meshes/walk.dae</filename>\n"
"    </animation>\n"
"</actor>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:54
msgid "Skin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:56
msgid ""
"In the `<skin>` tag we just loaded a COLLADA file `walk.dae` which specifies "
"how our actor will look. When a COLLADA file is used within the `<skin>` "
"tags its animation is loaded. The `<scale>` scales the skin's size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:58
msgid "Animation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:60
msgid ""
"In the `<animation>` tag we specify how our actor will move. We can combine "
"different skins with different animations as long as they have compatible "
"skeletons. Now run the world and we should see our model moving."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:62
msgid "![actor loaded with skin tag](tutorials/actors/actor_skin.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:62
msgid "actor loaded with skin tag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:64
msgid ""
"**Note**: You can find many actors and models on [Ignition Fuel](https://app."
"gazebosim.org/fuel)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:66
msgid "Scripted trajectory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:68
msgid ""
"This is the high level animation of actors, which consists of specifying a "
"series of poses to be reached at specific times. Gazebo Sim takes care of "
"interpolating the motion between them so the movement is fluid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:70
msgid ""
"Animations that have displacement on the X axis, like `walk.dae`, will have "
"the skeleton animated while following a trajectory. But for animations that "
"don't, such as `talk_b.dae`, their skeletons won't move if there's a "
"trajectory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:72
msgid ""
"We can make our actor follow the specified trajectory forever and start "
"playing as soon as the world is loaded as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:74
msgid ""
"<script>\n"
"    <loop>true</loop>\n"
"    <delay_start>0.000000</delay_start>\n"
"    <auto_start>true</auto_start>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:81
msgid "The script is defined between the `<actor>` `</actor>` tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:83
msgid "Inside the `<script>` tag the following parameters are available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:85
msgid ""
"`loop`: Set this to true for the script to be repeated in a loop. For a "
"fluid continuous motion, make sure the last waypoint matches the first one, "
"as we will do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:87
msgid ""
"`delay_start`: This is the time in seconds to wait before starting the "
"script. If running in a loop, this time will be waited before starting each "
"cycle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:89
msgid ""
"`auto_start`: Set to true if the animation should start as soon as the "
"simulation starts playing. It is useful to set this to false if the "
"animation should start playing only when triggered by a plugin, for example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:91
msgid "Let's define the trajectory as a sequence of waypoints:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:93
msgid ""
"        <trajectory id=\"0\" type=\"walk\">\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>0</time>\n"
"                <pose>0 0 1.0 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>2</time>\n"
"                <pose>2.0 0 1.0 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>2.5</time>\n"
"                <pose>2 0 1.0 0 0 1.57</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>4</time>\n"
"                <pose>2 2 1.0 0 0 1.57</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>4.5</time>\n"
"                <pose>2 2 1.0 0 0 3.142</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>6</time>\n"
"                <pose>0 2 1 0 0 3.142</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>6.5</time>\n"
"                <pose>0 2 1 0 0 -1.57</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>8</time>\n"
"                <pose>0 0 1.0 0 0 -1.57</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"            <waypoint>\n"
"                <time>8.5</time>\n"
"                <pose>0 0 1.0 0 0 0</pose>\n"
"            </waypoint>\n"
"        </trajectory>\n"
"    </script>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:135
msgid ""
"Within the `<trajectory>` tag we define a series of waypoints which our "
"actor will follow. The `<trajectory>` has two attributes: `id` and `type`. "
"The `type` should have the same name as the animation `walk`. Under the "
"`trajectory` tag we define the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:137
msgid ""
"`waypoint`: There can be any number of waypoints in a trajectory. Each "
"waypoint consists of a time and a pose:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:138
msgid ""
"`time`: The time in seconds, counted from the beginning of the script, when "
"the pose should be reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:139
msgid ""
"`pose`: The pose which should be reached with respect to the initial actor "
"pose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:141
msgid ""
"Run the world and we should see our actor moving in a square following the "
"waypoints."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:143
msgid "![actor_moving around](tutorials/actors/actor_demo.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:143
msgid "actor_moving around"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:145
msgid "**Notes**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:147
msgid ""
"The order in which waypoints are defined is not important, they will follow "
"the given times."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:148
msgid ""
"The trajectory is smoothed as a whole. This means that you'll get a fluid "
"motion, but the exact poses contained in the waypoints might not be reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../actors.md:150
msgid "Now it's your turn! Try out different trajectory descriptions."
msgstr ""

